   "source": [
    "Based on the information provided, here are some recommendations for including squared and cubed terms in my initial feature set:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. It's generally a good idea to include polynomial features like squared and cubed terms in my initial feature set, especially for regression problems. This allows the model to capture non-linear relationships between the features and the target variable.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. The polynomial features transform can create squared and cubed terms automatically for you. As mentioned in the search results, you can use scikit-learn's PolynomialFeatures class to generate these terms [1][2].\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Including polynomial terms can help reduce bias in the model and allow for interactions between features [6]. This may improve model performance, especially if there are non-linear relationships in my data.\n",
    "\n",
    "4. However, be cautious about adding too many polynomial terms, as this can dramatically increase the number of features and potentially lead to overfitting. Start with a lower degree (e.g. 2 or 3) and evaluate performance before increasing further [2].\n",
    "\n",
    "5. Remember that adding polynomial features will increase model complexity and computation time. Consider the trade-off between potential performance gains and increased complexity [5].\n",
    "\n",
    "6. After generating the polynomial features, you may want to use feature selection techniques to identify the most relevant features, as not all polynomial terms may be useful [5].\n",
    "\n",
    "7. Be sure to standardize my features before creating polynomial terms, as this can help with numerical stability and interpretation of the results [2].\n",
    "\n",
    "8. Include the polynomial feature generation step inside my cross-validation pipeline to avoid data leakage [5].\n",
    "\n",
    "In summary, including squared and cubed terms in my initial feature set is generally a good practice for capturing non-linear relationships. Use the PolynomialFeatures transform, start with a lower degree, and evaluate performance to determine if the additional complexity is beneficial for my specific problem.\n",
    "\n",
    "Citations:\n",
    "[1] https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/numerical-data/polynomial-transforms\n",
    "[2] https://machinelearningmastery.com/polynomial-features-transforms-for-machine-learning/\n",
    "[3] https://www.angela1c.com/projects/machine-learning-files/model1_polynomial/\n",
    "[4] https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s42452-024-05994-z\n",
